<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolaas Waterboer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1819–1896</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolaas Waterboer was kaptein (chief) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griquatown">Griquatown</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from 1852. When diamonds were found in the late 1860s, rival claims to the diggings were settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keate-award">Keate Award</a> of 1871 in his favour. He then accepted British protection, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the territory as Griqualand West, which included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> mines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolaas-waterboer.jpg" alt="Nicolaas Waterboer" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Microcosm. South African Library Anon. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaas_Waterboer_-_Griqua_leader_and_politician_of_the_Cape_Colony.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
